.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
compiles C sources, runs the preprocessor, emits assembly/object output, and
links via
.Xr as 1
and
.Xr ld 1 .
.Pp
When invoked as
.Nm cpp ,
preprocessor-only mode is selected.
.Pp
.Dv __BITINT_MAXWIDTH__
is 65535:
.Vt _BitInt Ns Pq Ar N
of any width up to it, and
.Li wb
and
.Li uwb
constants of any size, are laid out and passed as gcc does, with
multiplication, division and the floating conversions done by
libgcc's
.Fn __mulbitint3 ,
.Fn __divmodbitint4 ,
.Fn __floatbitint*
and
.Fn __fix*bitint ,
but for a product of two 64-bit limbs on x86-64
.Po
.Vt __int128 ,
.Vt _BitInt Ns Pq 65
to
.Vt _BitInt Ns Pq 128
.Pc ,
made inline with
.Li mulq ,
as gcc makes it.
.Vt __int128
and
.Vt unsigned __int128
exist on x86-64, where
.Dv __SIZEOF_INT128__
is 16; on i386 they are rejected and
.Dv __SIZEOF_INT128__
is left undefined, as gcc does.
.Pp
gcc's x86 named address spaces,
.Li __seg_fs
and
.Li __seg_gs ,
are type qualifiers: an object in one, through a pointer to one, a member
of one or declared in one, is read and written through
.Li %fs
or
.Li %gs ,
the address its offset there, and an inline asm memory operand of one is
printed with the segment, as Linux's per-CPU data needs.
Copying a structure into one is refused.
.Pp
.Li #pragma pack Ns Pq Ar n ,
.Li pack() ,
.Li pack(push Ns Op , Ar n Ns )
and
.Li pack(pop) ,
and the same through
.Li _Pragma ,
hold the members of the structures declared after them to alignment
.Ar n ,
as gcc does: one an
.Sq Li aligned
attribute asks more of and a bit-field's storage unit too, while a
structure's own
.Sq Li aligned
still counts.
.Pp
gcc's
.Sq Li counted_by Ns Pq Ar m
on a flexible array member or a pointer member names the member holding
its count, and
.Fn __builtin_counted_by_ref p->fam
is the address of that member, or a null
.Vt void *
where the member has none, as gcc gives it; no bounds are checked by it.
.Pp
Vector types, GNU
.Sq Li vector_size
and clang's
.Sq Li ext_vector_type ,
are laid out, operated on element by element, and passed and returned
as gcc (and clang) do: on i386 a 16-byte vector travels in
.Li %xmm0
to
.Li %xmm2
when the target has SSE, and an 8-byte one in an MMX register when it
has MMX, so code that calls gcc-built functions taking 8-byte vectors
must not rely on the x87 until the caller has issued
.Li emms ,
as
.Nm
does.
Without SSE a 16-byte vector is passed on the stack and returned in
memory, and without MMX an 8-byte one is too, aligned to 4 rather than
8, as gcc has them for that
.Fl march :
.Cm i686
and
.Cm pentiumpro
have neither,
.Cm pentium2
adds MMX,
.Cm pentium3
SSE.
.Pp
An object of
.Vt _Atomic
type of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes is read in one access, an 8-byte one on i386
too, and stored in one, sequentially consistently, and
.Li ++ ,
.Li --
and compound assignment on it are one atomic read-modify-write, a
compare-exchange loop; with the
.Fl march
that lacks the instruction, a call of
.Fn __atomic_compare_exchange_ Ns Ar N ,
.Fn __atomic_load_8
or
.Fn __atomic_store_8 .
gcc's
.In stdatomic.h
works as it is: its generic
.Fn __atomic_load ,
.Fn __atomic_store ,
.Fn __atomic_exchange ,
.Fn __atomic_compare_exchange ,
.Fn __atomic_test_and_set
and
.Fn __atomic_clear ,
and the
.Dv __GCC_ATOMIC_*_LOCK_FREE
values gcc gives each
.Fl march .
.Pp
.Vt _Decimal32 ,
.Vt _Decimal64
and
.Vt _Decimal128
hold the BID encoding gcc uses, and their arithmetic and conversions
call libgcc's
.Fn __bid_*
functions, so a program using them links only against a libgcc built
with decimal floating point (a Linux host's is; substrate's is not).
.Pp
Installed host-runnable copies of
.Nm ,
.Xr as 1 ,
and
.Xr ld 1
are typically staged with
.Dq Li make host_dist
and installed with
.Dq Li sudo make HOST_DIST_PREFIX=/opt/substrate host_install .
See
.Xr host_toolchain 7
for the full install and
.Ev PATH
workflow.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-pedantic-errors"
