With
.Cm thunk-extern ,
every indirect call and jump the compiler makes \(em through a function
pointer, or a
.Ic switch Ns 's
jump table \(em is a call or jump to
.Li __x86_indirect_thunk_ Ns Ar reg ,
the target in register
.Ar reg ;
the program supplies the thunks, as Linux does its retpolines.
With
.Cm keep ,
the default, they are the instructions.
.Xr gcc 1 Ns 's
.Cm thunk
and
.Cm thunk-inline ,
which make the thunks themselves, are refused.
.Fl mindirect-branch-register
is accepted: the target is always in a register.
